@auth
Feature: Registration, sign-in, and identity # features/auth.feature:2
  Converted from the Hiring_role and Working_role authentication and
  access-control browser specs. The browser assertions about dialogs and focus
  traps have no server-side meaning; what mattered - who gets a session, who is
  refused, and what the refusal says - is asserted here against the real API.
  @db
  Scenario: An employer signs in with the correct password           # features/auth.feature:10
    Given the mock data is in place                                  # features/steps/given_steps.py:22
    When a client signs in as the employer with the correct password # features/steps/when_steps.py:87
    Then the response status code is 200                             # features/steps/then_steps.py:43
    And the response field "token" is present                        # features/steps/then_steps.py:82

  @db
  Scenario: A worker signs in with the correct password            # features/auth.feature:17
    Given the mock data is in place                                # features/steps/given_steps.py:22
    When a client signs in as the worker with the correct password # features/steps/when_steps.py:87
    Then the response status code is 200                           # features/steps/then_steps.py:43
    And the response field "token" is present                      # features/steps/then_steps.py:82

  @db
  Scenario: The signed-in employer reads their own identity     # features/auth.feature:24
    Given the mock data is in place                             # features/steps/given_steps.py:22
    When the employer sends a GET request to "auth/me"          # features/steps/when_steps.py:48
    Then the response status code is 200                        # features/steps/then_steps.py:43
    And the response field "name" is "Demo Employer"            # features/steps/then_steps.py:67
    And the response field "email" is "employer@localhire.test" # features/steps/then_steps.py:67
    And the response field "role" is "Hiring"                   # features/steps/then_steps.py:67

  @db
  Scenario: The signed-in worker reads their own identity  # features/auth.feature:33
    Given the mock data is in place                        # features/steps/given_steps.py:22
    When the worker sends a GET request to "auth/me"       # features/steps/when_steps.py:48
    Then the response status code is 200                   # features/steps/then_steps.py:43
    And the response field "name" is "Demo Worker"         # features/steps/then_steps.py:67
    And the response field "role" is "LookingForWork"      # features/steps/then_steps.py:67
    And the response field "isProfileComplete" is "true"   # features/steps/then_steps.py:67

  @db
  Scenario: A wrong password is refused                                                                       # features/auth.feature:42
    Given the mock data is in place                                                                           # features/steps/given_steps.py:22
    When a client signs in with email "employer@localhire.test", password "WrongPassword1!" and role "Hiring" # features/steps/when_steps.py:80
    Then the response status code is 401                                                                      # features/steps/then_steps.py:43
    And the response body contains the error "Invalid email, password, or role"                               # features/steps/then_steps.py:194

  @db
  Scenario: An unknown email is refused with the same message as a wrong password                       # features/auth.feature:49
    Given the mock data is in place                                                                     # features/steps/given_steps.py:22
    When a client signs in with email "nobody@localhire.test", password "LocalHire1!" and role "Hiring" # features/steps/when_steps.py:80
    Then the response status code is 401                                                                # features/steps/then_steps.py:43
    And the response body contains the error "Invalid email, password, or role"                         # features/steps/then_steps.py:194

  @db
  Scenario: Signing in with the wrong role for an existing account is refused                                     # features/auth.feature:56
